NAME
       ps - list the running processes

SYNOPSIS
       ps [OPTIONS]

DESCRIPTION
       ps prints one line for each process: its process ID (PID), the
       terminal it belongs to, how much processor time it has used, and the
       command. Unlike top it shows the list once and exits, so its output
       can be sorted, filtered with grep or used in scripts.

       ps understands two styles of options. Unix style options start with a
       dash and can be grouped (ps -ef). BSD style options have no dash
       (ps aux). They mean different things: "ps -a" and "ps a" are not the
       same. With no options ps shows only the processes of your own
       terminal.

OPTIONS
       -e, -A
              Every process on the system.

       -f     Full format: UID, PID, PPID (parent), C, STIME, TTY, TIME and
              the whole command line.

       -l     Long format, with the priority (PRI) and nice value (NI).

       -u USER, -U USER
              Only the processes of USER (a name or a UID; several separated
              by commas).

       -p PID, --pid PID
              Only these process IDs.

       --ppid PID
              Only the children of these parent processes.

       -C NAME
              Only the processes whose command name is NAME.

       -o FIELDS, --format FIELDS
              Choose the columns, for example -o pid,user,%cpu,%mem,comm.
              Write FIELD= (with nothing after the =) to leave out the header.
              Useful fields: pid, ppid, user, uid, ni (nice), pri, %cpu,
              %mem, rss, vsz, stat, tty, time, etime, start, comm (command
              name), args (whole command line), label (SELinux context).

       --sort [+|-]FIELD[,...]
              Sort by these fields; a minus sign sorts from biggest to
              smallest. ps --sort=-%cpu puts the busiest processes first.

       -Z, --context
              Add a column with each process's SELinux context (type such as
              httpd_t or sshd_t).

       --no-headers
              Leave out the header line.

       a, x, u (BSD style)
              "a" shows processes of all users that have a terminal, "x" adds
              those without a terminal, "u" uses the user-oriented format
              (USER, PID, %CPU, %MEM, VSZ, RSS, TTY, STAT, START, TIME,
              COMMAND). Together: ps aux. Add Z for the SELinux context:
              ps auxZ.

STAT
       The STAT column letters: R running, S sleeping, D waiting for disk
       (cannot be interrupted), T stopped, Z zombie (finished, but its parent
       has not collected it); extra letters: < high priority (negative
       nice), N low priority (positive nice), s session leader, + in the
       foreground of its terminal.

EXAMPLES
       Everything, in two common styles:

              $ ps -ef
              $ ps aux

       The five processes using the most CPU, then the most memory:

              $ ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -6
              $ ps -eo pid,user,%mem,comm --sort=-%mem | head -6

       The nice value of one process, and all processes of student:

              $ ps -o pid,ni,comm -p 1234
              $ ps -u student

       SELinux contexts of the web server's processes:

              # ps -eZ | grep httpd

       Only the PIDs of sshd, without a header:

              $ ps -C sshd -o pid=

SEE ALSO
       top(1), pgrep(1), kill(1), pkill(1), nice(1), renice(1), pidof(1)
